## Four Proposed Use Cases

Option 1: extract pricing data from PDFs, Excel, and slides into a standard template, flagging missing values.

Option 2: SLM agent as QA assistant around an existing insurance ML model (input errors, confidence, missing data).

Option 3: check whether claims in insurance complaints are supported by policy wording or FCA material via RAG.

Main challenge: building a reliable labeled benchmark without private data

Option 4: detect complaint trends over time from FOS data; risks are labeling consistency and selection bias.



## Prototypes

Option 1 prototype used 60 synthetic files (20 PDFs, 20 Excels, 20 slides) in four layouts, with Qwen 2.5 1.5B locally.

Option 2 prototype on public motorcycle policy data (15,000 synthetic records) underperformed fixed checks.

Zero-shot LLM ran in about 20 minutes vs over one hour for the fixed pipeline

Tushar Sankhe praised the UK-grounded research but said extraction leads to OCR/VLM benchmarking, off-target for this project.



## Recommended Direction from Tushar Sankhe

- Compare two or three base models (e.g. Qwen, Mistral), justify the pick, then fine-tune with QLoRA SFT and compare accuracy.
    - Consider LLM as a judge; check hallucination and bias too
    - Qwen 2.5 3B showed good gains before; 7B too heavy for a laptop

- Build data by scraping UK motor insurance policies and expanding 15,000 records to about 50,000 via synthetic generators.
    - e.g. NVIDIA's free synthetic data generator; keep distributions the same; no client data

- Aim to go beyond the previous intern's vehicle insurance work, focusing on inquiries, complaints, and claims.

- Results could become a white paper jointly reviewed by mentors and shared with the open source community.



